## Context

A pasted link is often the first thing anyone sees of aidemd.dev. A scraper — Slackbot, the X and LinkedIn crawlers, Apple's link preview — fetches the page, reads its share tags, downloads the image and composes the preview before anyone clicks. None of them runs JavaScript or waits on a slow image service, and they cache what they fetch, Meta by image address. The card is therefore a finished image already sitting on the site.

Each platform shows a different slice of the preview. X's large card shows the image with only the title laid over its bottom edge; LinkedIn drops the description; Slack shrinks the image to roughly a third of its size beside the text. On most surfaces the image is the one part of the preview the site fully controls, so it has to say which page the link points to on its own, and still say it when drawn small.

The site has two kinds of pages to preview. Home, /brain and /privacy are about the site as a whole, and the site card — Expo beside the tagline — speaks for them. The docs pages, section indexes and /learn pages are what people actually quote and pass along, and a preview showing the site card for one of them names the wrong thing. /observer has its own screenshot preview and is not this module's.

## Strategy

**Draw every card from one design with two layouts: the site card and the page card.** The page card keeps the site card's frame, palette, typeface and AIDEMD.DEV identity and gives its main space to the page's title and, where it has one, its section. One design means one place to change copy, tokens or layout, and a reader moving between previews sees one site. Home, /brain and /privacy get the site card; /observer is never drawn.

**Take the page list and every word on a card only from the page records handed in.** The site's one page list already holds each page's title, section and share-image address; reading it is what keeps a card from naming another page or an old title. The module does not walk content, guess a section or shorten a title, and the same records always produce the same cards, so a rebuild changes only the cards whose page changed.

**Render all cards at build time, when the pre-deploy build step calls.** That step already writes every machine-read file from the same page list, so cards are redrawn on every deploy from the titles about to ship and can never trail them. Scrapers get finished images, never a runtime service. Only the card itself is captured; the design file and its notes stay out of the published site.

**Fit long titles by wrapping first, then stepping the size down to a fixed floor, and fail the build if a title still does not fit.** A card must read at Slack's shrunken size and under X's title strip along the bottom edge, so text keeps a safe margin from every edge, and the title is the largest thing on the card. A title clipped or shrunk past reading would ship silently; a failed build gets fixed.

**Check every card after drawing it: a true PNG, exactly 1200×630, under 300 KB.** X quietly drops an oversized image to its small card, and some consumers crop or reject other sizes or formats, so an out-of-bounds card is a broken preview even when it looks fine on disk. Any card that fails the check stops the build rather than shipping.

**Keep drawing deterministic: the site's own palette, its one monospace typeface loaded from the repo, Expo by name.** A typeface fetched over the network can silently fall back to another font, reflowing every title. Each card's alt text names its page, and where Expo appears it is called Expo, because screen readers and crawlers read that text even though sighted readers never do.

## Good examples

**A docs page shared in Slack.** The preview's thumbnail shows the warm-dark frame with the AIDEMD.DEV identity, a small "methodology" section label, and the page title "aide-spec" large enough to read at Slack's shrunken width. The title beside it in Slack and the title on the card agree, and the page clicked through to carries the same title.

**A long /learn title.** "Spec-driven development vs intent-driven development" wraps to three lines at a size that still reads at thumbnail width, clear of the bottom band where X lays its title strip, with nothing clipped.

**/brain shared on LinkedIn.** The preview shows the site card — Expo beside the tagline — because /brain is about the site as a whole and the page list hands it the site card.

## Bad examples

**One page showing another page's card.** A /learn page's preview shows the agents-section card because two records were matched by position instead of by page, or a renamed docs page still unfurls with its old title because its card was not redrawn. Every card is a valid PNG and passes the size check; the preview still sends a reader to the wrong thing.

**A title readable only at full size.** A long docs title is shrunk until it fits, past the floor, so it passes the dimension check and looks fine opened full size, but in Slack's thumbnail and under X's title strip it is an unreadable grey band.

**A card drawn in a fallback font.** The typeface failed to load during the render and a system monospace stood in; every title reflows, two clip at the right edge, and the cards no longer match the site a reader clicks through to.

